Original commit body from af818e4b757ad6a7a267e44f32ebcf809f0ace16:

INV-vavup, the owner-decided route to INV-dabuf, plus the predicate defect it
exposed (INV-potuf). Both land together because shipping the first without the
second is a false confirm -- measured, below.

PART 1 -- REDIRECTION (INV-vavup)

bash.py dispatched on function_definition / declaration_command / command
only, so redirection emitted NOTHING. Measured on an 8-statement script of the
common idioms: 5 edges, every one a command_launch, and zero for the three
writes to host paths. The launch surface was emitted; the write surface was
invisible. `echo "$SECRET" > /etc/cron.d/pwned` is the same cron-dropper
INV-larol was filed about, written with a builtin and a redirect instead of
`curl -o`, and it went straight through.

ADR-0016 NEVER FORBADE THIS, and I misread it twice before measuring. It rules
out attributing a LAUNCHED program's I/O to the script that ran it -- correct
for `curl -o file`, where curl performs the write. `echo x > file` is the OTHER
surface: the shell itself opens and writes the target (echo is a builtin, and
even for an external command the redirection is established before exec), so
fs_write is exactly right -- the standing os.remove has in python.

Usual split: the ANALYZER emits an edge for the construct, the CATALOGUE
classifies the operator, on the shipped module_attr_ref precedent (os.environ
is an attribute access, not a call, and reaches the pipeline the same way).
Putting the operator in DATA is what lets a divergent shell (tcsh, fish) get
different rows without touching the analyzer -- the conserved-set framing.
`>` and `>>` are ONE primitive at two modes, truncate/append carried on
io_mode rather than separate boundary rows that could collide under INV-zumin.
A variable target (`> "$OUT"`) emits UNRESOLVED rather than skipping: a silent
skip is the fail-open direction this area keeps paying for. Pipes are
deliberately OUT -- `a | b` is process topology, not a filesystem write.

PART 2 -- BOTH ENDS OF A FLOW (INV-potuf)

The census marked a language taint-unsupported only when it had NEITHER
sources NOR sinks. One-sided coverage makes propagation structurally
impossible, so it guarantees ZERO findings -- and zero findings is what
`confirmed` is made of. The predicate now requires both, agreeing with the
propagation loop three lines away already guarded on
`if per_lang_sources and per_lang_sinks`.

It became reachable when io_primitives stopped being purely a BOUNDARY
catalogue: the shipped taint_sinks/ tree was retired (51e1d232f3) and sinks
are auto-derived from io_primitives via AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP. So Part 1's
boundary catalogue silently minted 3 bash SINKS. Measured on the shipped CLI,
a script whose only statement is `echo "$API_KEY" > /etc/cron.d/pwned`:

  taint_flow claim   before: confirmed   rc 0     <- FALSE CONFIRM
                     after:  inconclusive rc 2
  boundary claim     after:  violated    rc 1     <- Part 1's win, intact

Worse than the flip: under the old predicate the stderr note AND the
unsupported_taint_languages field both went silent, so a DISCLOSED false
confirm became an undisclosed one.

USER-VISIBLE CHANGE BEYOND BASH, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER:
TYPESCRIPT taint claims that read `confirmed` today read `inconclusive` after
this. Typescript has 6 hand-written sources and 0 sinks -- sinks bucket by
`catalog.language` and _CATALOG_ALIASES maps typescript->javascript, so its
rows would land under javascript while its sources key under `typescript`.
The two halves disagree about the language's name. That channel is LIVE ON DEV
TODAY and predates this work: with bash.yaml removed, bash is 0/0 and
unsupported under either predicate while typescript STILL flips. Filed as
INV-potuf; its coverage gap is NOT fixed here (resolve to javascript's sinks /
give it own rows / retire the alias is a real three-way decision).

HOW THIS WAS ADJUDICATED. A three-judge panel plus two blind measurement
passes, and they overturned me twice. I had concluded "must not ship"; the
correct statement is "must not ship WITHOUT the predicate fix in the same PR",
because the defect is SINK-ONLY support -- strictly worse than mis-support,
since it looks analysable while guaranteeing zero findings. I had also
suspected my own A/B was confounded between bash.py and bash.yaml; it was not,
and the refutation came by a route I had not seen (the failing test builds a
synthetic behavior map via --input, so the analyzer never runs). Two judges
then disagreed about collateral; two blind passes over the full 89-language
taxonomy settled it -- typescript and bash are the ONLY two affected, all 14
other catalogued languages carry both halves, and both flips are toward FEWER
confirmed verdicts.

FOUR OF MY OWN ERRORS, each caught by measurement rather than reasoning, each
green on the unit tests: the YAML used `name:` where the schema wants
`functions:` (catalogue loaded 0 primitives, tagged nothing); the analyzer
first decided write-vs-read itself, which defeats the point of operator-in-data;
the template declared `status: in_progress` (overlays MUST be `overlay` -- it
errors) and grouped commands under an invented `module: launch` that matches
NOTHING and fails silently; and the redirect branch read a `current_function`
the command branch happened to leave behind, so attribution depended on
tree-walk order. mypy caught the last one.

The shipped template is now its own positive control: loaded through
load_catalog it matches, and the union result documents the thing users most
need to know -- a row ADDS a claim without removing the launch opacity
(io_boundary stays command_launch, io_boundaries gains the declared one), so a
verdict resting on a launch stays qualified at rc 3, never clean.

Ref: INV-vavup, INV-potuf, INV-dabuf, INV-larol, INV-zumin, ADR-0016.
